        "description": "<p>Shortly after Apple launched the budget <a href=\"https://github.com/geerlingguy/sbc-reviews/issues/102\">MacBook Neo</a>, Dell announced their response, a new low-end <a href=\"https://www.dell.com/en-us/shop/dell-laptops/new-xps-13-laptop/spd/xps13dx13260laptop\">XPS 13</a>.</p>\n<figure class=\"insert-image\"><img src=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/blog/2026/excited-for-intel-efficiency/dell-xps-13-running-fedora.jpeg\"\n\t\t\talt=\"Dell XPS 13 running Fedora 44\" width=\"700\" height=\"auto\">\n</figure>\n\n<p>Matching the Neo's <em>current</em> pricing, it starts at $699, or $599 with an educational discount. That discount is currently set to expire on November 2, and with the current component pricing insanity, I'd be surprised if we don't see a price increase on both laptops by next year.</p>\n<p>I ran the XPS 13 through my <a href=\"https://github.com/geerlingguy/sbc-reviews/issues/111\">gauntlet of benchmarks</a>, and published a review on my YouTube channel:</p>",
